{"format":"helia-content","version":1,"id":"recursive-coalgebra","title":{"Inline":[{"Text":"Recursive coalgebra"}]},"title_text":"Recursive coalgebra","kind":"definition","taxon":null,"url":"recursive-coalgebra.html","content":{"Block":[{"Group":{"role":"env","attrs":[["entry","recursive-coalgebra"],["kind","definition"],["label","Definition"],["numbered","true"]],"body":{"Block":[{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"Fix an endofunctor "},{"Math":{"src":"F : cal(C) -> cal(C)"}},{"Text":". A "},{"EntryLink":{"id":"coalgebras-displayed","label":null,"body":[{"Text":"coalgebra"}]}},{"Text":" "},{"Math":{"src":"gamma : X -> F X"}},{"Text":" is "},{"Emph":[{"Text":"recursive"}]},{"Text":" when for every "},{"EntryLink":{"id":"algebras-displayed","label":null,"body":[{"Text":"algebra"}]}},{"Text":" "},{"Math":{"src":"alpha : F B -> B"}},{"Text":" there is exactly one "},{"EntryLink":{"id":"hylomorphism","label":null,"body":[{"Text":"hylomorphism"}]}},{"Text":" "},{"Math":{"src":"h : X -> B"}},{"Text":" from "},{"Math":{"src":"gamma"}},{"Text":" to "},{"Math":{"src":"alpha"}},{"Text":", that is, exactly one solution of"}]},{"Math":{"src":"h = gamma ⋆ F h\n⋆ alpha ."}},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"Equivalently, the functor "},{"Math":{"src":"sans(\"Hylo\")(gamma, -)"}},{"Text":" of the "},{"EntryLink":{"id":"hylomorphism-profunctor","label":null,"body":[{"Text":"hylomorphism profunctor"}]}},{"Text":" is constantly a singleton."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"Recursiveness is a coalgebraic form of well-foundedness: "},{"Math":{"src":"gamma"}},{"Text":" decomposes each input into subproblems, and recursiveness says that every divide-and-conquer program built on this decomposition has a unique meaning, without mentioning an order on inputs. "},{"Cite":{"key":"alexandruIntrinsicallyCorrectAlgorithms2025-preprint","supplement":null,"rendered":"[1]"}},{"Text":" use recursive coalgebras on categories of indexed families to obtain algorithms that are correct by the type of the map they compute."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Strong":[{"Text":"Example."}]},{"Text":" If "},{"Math":{"src":"(mu F, sans(\"in\"))"}},{"Text":" is an "},{"EntryLink":{"id":"initial-algebra","label":null,"body":[{"Text":"initial algebra"}]}},{"Text":", then "},{"Math":{"src":"sans(\"in\")"}},{"Text":" is invertible (Lambek’s lemma) and "},{"Math":{"src":"sans(\"in\")^(-1) : mu F -> F (mu\nF)"}},{"Text":" is a recursive coalgebra. Precomposing with the isomorphism "},{"Math":{"src":"sans(\"in\")"}},{"Text":", the equation "},{"Math":{"src":"h = sans(\"in\")^(-1) ⋆ F h ⋆ alpha"}},{"Text":" is equivalent to "},{"Math":{"src":"sans(\"in\") ⋆ h = F h ⋆ alpha"}},{"Text":", which says "},{"Math":{"src":"h"}},{"Text":" is an algebra map out of the initial algebra; there is exactly one, "},{"Math":{"src":"sans(\"fold\") thin alpha"}},{"Text":"."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"The dual notion is a "},{"EntryLink":{"id":"corecursive-algebra","label":null,"body":[{"Text":"corecursive algebra"}]}},{"Text":"."}]}]}}}]}}
